**STATUS 2026-09-15: DRAFT — machine-drafted, persona-reviewed three times, revised six times against those rounds and three live fact-checks; Meng has not read the post. Citations checked live 2026-09-14; the third fact-check's corrections applied 2026-09-15 (notes 2, 3, 6, 11, 12, 22). Round 2 moved the literature to the notes and the first-reader paragraph to the close. Three NEEDS MENG markers remain, all in the notes, none answered on record: the payments pilot's scene (note 2), a particular of the CALC exchange (note 8), the two reviewing executives (note 18).**

A four-panel comic from 2016. One developer tells another that some day we won't even need coders any more: write a comprehensive and precise specification, and the program writes itself. Does he know the industry term for a specification that precise? Two balloons. "Code." "It's called code." A mug tips and the drink spills. CommitStrip published it in English as "A very comprehensive and precise spec," and the image file on their server still carries the French title it was drawn under: *Les specs c'est du code*.[^1]

We have spent six years running the joke backwards, on documents people had signed. An insurance policy is a specification: it says, in English, what is paid and when. In 2023 a major insurer — millions of customers; it stays unnamed — had our group at Singapore Management University write one of its policies in L4, the programming language for law we build. The insurer's own claims adjusters walked us through it. The payout formula had two readings. The adjusters had been paying the larger one. That was delicate to report, because the adjusters were the people who could be penalized for overpaying, so we took it upstairs as a defect in the wording. When it reached the executives the conversation was about liability. As I recall it, and the slides are lost, nobody said which reading future claims would be paid on. The fix went into the wording of the term.[^2]

A commercial agreement is a specification too. For a payments company we wrote contracts dense with fee tables in L4 and served them to the company's own systems through a query interface, so that the contract itself answered what the fee on a transaction was.[^2] Neither engagement generated a program from a specification. Both specifications were already programs. One had a bug.

The joke was old in 2016. In January 1985 Bertrand Meyer catalogued the faults of a specification written in careful English by careful people. He found seven kinds. Two of them are the ones a policyholder meets. *Ambiguity* is a sentence with two readings, which is what the payout formula had. *Contradiction* is two sentences that cannot both be obeyed, which is what a data-breach law had when one of our early pilots found a case where a firm was obliged to notify its users and advised not to.[^3] Meyer's remedy was a formal notation.

In the summer of 1983 a group at Imperial College London typed a large part of the British Nationality Act 1981 into Prolog. In January 1984 they showed it to Home Office officials involved in drafting the act. Their 1986 paper says an executable representation "can be used not only to apply the rules, but to aid the process of drafting and redrafting the rules in the first place — a point that was made by Layman Allen as long ago as 1957."[^4] It records the demonstration and no adoption.[^5] Law got there first. Forty years on, the drafter's tool is still a word processor.

## Why leave English

The government version of the wish is called rules as code. The name is the New Zealand Better Rules team's; a 2020 primer from the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) defines it as "a machine-consumable version of some types of government rules, to exist alongside the existing natural language counterpart"; and its drafting-office arm is in Jersey, where Matthew Waddington's Computer-Readable Legislation Project has been working with our group.[^6] In an interview in February 2025 he said what such a tool is for: when the computer finds a logical error in a draft, "I can now fix it before that draft becomes final and gets enacted." Hand the job to a large language model (LLM) instead, and "you have to accept that you will have no way of being sure whether it has done it right."[^6]

In July 2026, at the Commonwealth Association of Legislative Counsel (CALC) conference in Singapore, a senior legislative drafter with deep roots in the rules as code movement asked me why leave English at all. If the goal is an editor that flags every ambiguity and every drafting error as the draft is typed, why not build it over the English the drafter already writes? Why not push Logical English, Prolog that reads as English sentences, from Robert Kowalski of the 1983 Imperial group and three colleagues, further in that direction?[^7] I said that such an editor ends somewhere specific. A checker that has to be sure of every sentence makes the drafter write in the subset of English it can be sure of, and that subset is a language with a grammar, what linguists call a controlled natural language (CNL). Between that language and L4, I said, there is no daylight.[^8]

A spell-checker underlines a word and imposes no vocabulary on you. A form that will not submit until the date is written one way does. A checker that only flags things restricts nothing; what matters is which errors are on the list. The ones a drafter wants caught are the ones it took a formalization to find: two limbs that cannot both hold, a term used before it is defined, a proviso that binds nothing, an "and" a court could read as "or." Each is a fact about the sentence's logical structure, which the checker must first recover, and a parser recovering structure from unrestricted legal English will sometimes be unsure. Then it either guesses, and a guess fails silently, or asks the drafter to rewrite until it is sure. A drafter who rewrites until the parser is sure is writing in the language the parser is sure about.

Tobias Kuhn counted a hundred such languages in 2014, "a single scattered cloud filling the conceptual space between natural languages such as English on the one end and formal languages such as propositional logic on the other."[^9] Our bet is the comic's: a specification precise enough to check is code, and an editor rigorous enough to be worth having ends in a language. What would falsify it is a checker that catches a deontic contradiction over unrestricted enacted English, reliably enough to draft against. Deontic is the logician's word for the logic of must and may; the contradiction is an obligation and a prohibition that apply to the same facts. Nobody has shown one. We would like to be shown.

The argument to a drafting office is for the class. The instance is section 1(5A) of the British Nationality Act 1981, from the encoding in our repository, beside the words it encodes.[^10] The statute:

> (5A) Those requirements are that on the date on which the order is made or the Convention adoption is effected (as the case may be)—
> (a) the adopter or, in the case of a joint adoption, one of the adopters is a British citizen; and
> (b) in a case within subsection (5)(b), the adopter or, in the case of a joint adoption, both of the adopters are habitually resident in the United Kingdom or in a designated territory.

The L4:

```
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5A) — the requirements are met on the date of the order or adoption` adoption MEANS
        "(5A) Those requirements are that on the date on which the order is made or the Convention adoption is effected (as the case may be)—"
    ... `1(5A)(a) — the adopter, or one joint adopter, a British citizen` adoption
    AND `1(5A)(b) — habitual residence, in a Convention case` adoption
```

That is the surface of an L4 rule: what it looks like on the page. The uppercase words are the language's own, what the rule takes (GIVEN), what it gives (GIVETH), its definition (MEANS). Everything in backticks is the statute's vocabulary, a whole phrase used as a name, so that the rule reads as the clause it encodes; the line in quotation marks is the statute's own opening words, pasted in and read past. The three dots are an unspoken *and*, three dots for three letters, so that the limbs line up in a column as a drafter lists them. Two dots are an unspoken *or*; the (b) limb, a few lines up in the same file, uses them to render its proviso as "not a Convention case, or habitually resident."[^10]

The difference from Logical English is what happens to a mistake. Every L4 rule has a type, declared or inferred, and the compiler checks every rule's type against every other's before anything runs, so a rule applied to the wrong kind of thing is refused at the desk. Logical English runs as Prolog, which has no such step: the rule runs and comes back with no answer. Catala, a language for statutes from Inria and Microsoft Research, interleaves each article of law with a rule in code, so the lawyer reads the article and then, beneath it, a program. L4 makes the rule the text the lawyer reads, with the statute's words inside it, and compiles out to Catala among others.[^11]

## The same wish, from the other side

The software industry arrived at the same wish from the other side, and fast. In February 2025 Andrej Karpathy named "a new kind of coding I call 'vibe coding', where you fully give in to the vibes, embrace exponentials, and forget that the code even exists."[^13] By September GitHub had an open-source toolkit for it and a thesis in a line: "We're moving from 'code is the source of truth' to 'intent is the source of truth.'"[^14] The promise is older than the tools, and the customers who needed the ceiling wrote code.[^15]

Statutory reasoning, applying a statute to the facts of a case, has had a benchmark since 2020.[^16] James Wiles, in a preprint of August 2026, had GPT-4 translate four English legal statements into a deontic formalism and found three of them "accepted as correct; actually wrong." Four statements, one model: a demonstration.[^17] One was a rule with a disjunctive condition: anyone not wearing a tie, or blond, must leave the room. The generated check "returns True for every input," and three of its four tests expected True, so the suite looked healthy. It ran. It read plausibly. It ordered everyone out of the room. "Neither GPT-4 nor I noticed the bug at the time," Wiles writes; his name for the class is "code that runs, reads plausibly, but silently encodes the wrong norm."[^17]

Neural tools translate well enough that our own draft for the International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Law (ICAIL) uses a large language model for exactly that: to draft a first formalization, and to explain a result in prose.[^18] The difference is where the draft lands. Wiles's fix is building blocks suggested inline as the lawyer drafts, "the way Copilot and grammar checkers work today"; in his sketch the English draft is a prompt, and re-editing it re-runs a fallible translator with nothing to catch a guard that returns True for every input.[^17] In Copilot's the suggestion is the artifact, in the file that ships, and what makes that survivable is everything around it: a test suite, a type checker, a reviewer who reads the diff.

So what does a language a compiler checks buy against a wrong norm that is well-formed? Less than the word "compiler" suggests. Type-checking catches the ill-formed, a name used and never defined. It cannot catch a rule that is well-typed and wrong, which is Wiles's class. The ICAIL draft says so: the gap "between an isomorphic formalisation and a *faithful* one … is not something the language can close on its own; it remains, as it should, a matter for human legal judgement, now better supported by tools." Isomorphic means that the formal text follows the statute clause by clause, so the two can be read side by side.[^18] What catches the wrong norm is a reader with the two texts in front of them. The surface above is engineered so that the reader can be the lawyer.

The draft's claim is that non-technical business stakeholders "are able to *read* an L4 ruleset and *sign off* on it as a faithful statement of the rules they intend." What I can put behind that sentence is two engagements, the data-breach rules of 2022 and the insurance policy of 2023. In each, the client assigned a business executive to review the encoding; neither was a programmer, and each reviewed it and signed off. Two readers, one per engagement, and no procedure on record. That is a qualitative impression, not a finding. The study that would turn it into one has not been done; its shape is the study a University of Michigan group reported in January 2022, recruiting programmers and lawyers to each build a tool that applies a statute to a layperson's case, and comparing what they built.[^18] A test would have caught Wiles's rule too; his suite had one discriminating case, in its first row, and nobody looked. These encodings have no test suite.

## What does not transfer

In software the code is a build output and the specification may outlive it; one framework's bet in 2025 is that "the specification itself becomes the maintained artifact, rather than the code."[^14] In law the text is the operative artifact. A signed contract is not regenerated from a specification, and a court reads the words.[^19] The price is L4's insistence on isomorphism, the word Bench-Capon and Coenen argued for in 1992 for "the well defined correspondence of the knowledge base to the source texts."[^20] The formal layer is a projection of the binding text, and isomorphism is what lets a reader audit it. Nothing in our software checks it. Our own appendix says a translation "earns the word *isomorphic* only if a reader can lay the two texts side by side and check them clause by clause."[^18] Law's structural advantage is that the prose must be read. The encoding is what goes stale.

Rules engines of that kind are sold to the agencies that administer a law; Oracle has owned one since 2008.[^21] They are bought after enactment, a second artifact the office that wrote the statute never sees, and they drift the moment the statute is amended and the rulebase is not. That is why the drafting office, which could write the rule once, in one artifact, matters out of proportion to its headcount.

The surface's affordances are designed and have not been user-tested; the ICAIL draft names "a controlled study of authorability" as future work, and the design note for the planned paper on the surface says in terms that the affordances are "designed, not yet user-tested."[^22] Kuhn's scattered cloud is what a field looks like when no one has yet made a controlled language that people write in unprompted, and the sign-off above is an anecdote until it is a study. A stakeholder who signs off believes the ruleset says what they intend, and that belief is what a study would have to test; programmers in a randomized trial of 2025 believed AI tools had sped them up after the measurement showed them slower.[^23]

The person on the other end of the payout formula had signed the policy without reading it, which is most of us. He was the first reader I had in mind, unsure of his position under the law or under a handful of policies he signed without understanding them; the lawyer came later. The data-breach pilot ended in a web wizard for exactly him, and it is still online: enter the facts of your situation, get back what you must do, each answer citing the clause it came from. It covers one obligation under one Singapore statute. Every encoding in these pilots was written by hand, so he cannot yet upload his lease. What stands between him and that is a run of the encoding pipeline over the law his lease sits under, about $20 of a Claude Code subscription per statute by my own reckoning, research included, and a lawyer's review of the result before anyone relies on it.[^12] What the surface above gives him meanwhile is a page that a person who does not program can hold against the policy and check.

Mireille Hildebrandt wrote in 2020: "What code-driven law does is to fold enactment, interpretation and application into one stroke, collapsing the distance between legislator, executive and court. It has to foresee all potential scenarios and develop sub-rules that hopefully cover all future interactions …"[^24] Her objection is to code that replaces the instrument, and a compile error a drafter fixes on Tuesday binds no court on Thursday. But an encoding beside a statute will still be the version that gets consulted, and what it did not foresee will look like what the statute did not provide for. Isomorphism is our answer to that, a discipline kept by a reader, and it guarantees nothing. The paper planned with Adam Wyner for the workshop on Controlled Natural Language puts the lawyer in the reader's seat, and proposes to evaluate the surface above through Green and Petre's Cognitive Dimensions of Notations, a vocabulary for what a notation affords and what it costs.[^22] The drafter who asked why leave English is the reader that paper is for. Nothing in the comic says who reads the code. The mug is in the hand of the developer who made the claim, and it is tipping.

---

Next: what the compiler checks once the text is in.

