nd Adam Wyner, <https://docs.oasis-open.org/legalruleml/legalruleml-core-spec/v1.0/legalruleml-core-spec-v1.0.html>; and OASIS *Akoma Ntoso* 1.0 Part 1 (OASIS Standard, 29 August 2018), <https://docs.oasis-open.org/legaldocml/akn-core/v1.0/akn-core-v1.0-part1-vocabulary.html> (all three pages checked 2026-09-14 for title, status and date only).

[^8]: The exchange is told from a project working note of 2026-09-12, `cnl-lowcode-background-reading` in the project's Claude memory records, written by a session that day, which records Meng's side verbatim: an editor pushed "far enough to achieve your goals of allowing a realtime editor component to flag every ambiguity and every drafting error that you want drafters to learn to avoid" ends "with a CNL: a natural language restricted sufficiently to allow that degree of rigour; and between that language and L4 there is no daylight" ("rigour" is the note's spelling; the words before the first fragment name a particular prototype and are left out). The body's paraphrase, and its gloss of a CNL as the subset of English a checker can be sure of, a language with a grammar, are ours; "no daylight" is the one phrase carried over as said. The note paraphrases the other side of the exchange — that a drafter could stay in natural language and get the same checks from language processing in a tight loop. The note heads the exchange with its own date, and that heading is superseded. Meng, 2026-09-14, answering the mark this note carried: attribute the question to a "senior legislative drafter with deep roots in the Rules as Code movement"; "The conversation actually occurred circa 2026-07-15 at the CALC (Commonwealth Association of Legislative Counsel) conference in Singapore, and was recorded in session on 2026-09-14." The conference ran 15–17 July 2026 (calc.ngo and calc2026.agc.gov.sg, checked 2026-09-15), which is what the body's "July 2026" rests on. The questioner is described and not named, as Meng ruled; the note's account of the exchange has not been checked with its other party. [NEEDS MENG: one particular of the exchange — where at the conference it happened, what was in front of you, what the drafter said in reply; the body has the argument and no room.]

[^9]: Tobias Kuhn, "A Survey and Classification of Controlled Natural Languages," *Computational Linguistics* 40(1):121–170 (2014), DOI 10.1162/COLI_a_00168. PDF read at <https://attempto.ifi.uzh.ch/site/pubs/papers/kuhn2014cl.pdf> (checked 2026-09-14). "100 languages from 1930 until today" and "single scattered cloud" are from the abstract; his "Definition 2 (short)" is "a constructed language that is based on a certain natural language, being more restrictive concerning lexicon, syntax, and/or semantics while preserving most of its natural properties," of which the body's "the subset of English a checker can be sure of" is our paraphrase, not his wording; Basic English (Ogden 1930) is the earliest entry.

[^10]: `jl4/examples/legal/bna/bna.l4` in this repository, lines 548–554, copied verbatim; the file's goldens are in `jl4/examples/legal/bna/tests/` and `l4 check jl4/examples/legal/bna/bna.l4` printed "Check succeeded" on 2026-09-14 with the installed binary (built 27 August). The (b) limb is at lines 540–546 of the same file, with the comment "Drafting pattern: NOT X .. Y." The statute text is quoted from `jl4/examples/legal/bna/source-s1.txt`, the file's own provenance copy of the legislation.gov.uk revised text of s 1 as retrieved 2026-08-02, with the source's amendment markers ("[F16 …]") dropped as the encoding's header says it drops them. The description of the devices follows `paper/icail/l4-icail.tex`: "Two registers" ("the same typographic convention by which COBOL and SQL set their keywords apart from the programmer's own identifiers"), "Indentation, not parentheses," and "Inert scaffolding and asyndeton" (from line 627): three dots and two dots as "lightweight infix synonyms for AND and OR," with "three for the three letters of AND, two for the two of OR, so that in the fixed-width register the implicit mark and its spelled-out form occupy the same column," and "a quoted string in a boolean position is treated as inert: it evaluates to TRUE inside an AND and to FALSE inside an OR" — which is what "counts for nothing" renders. The tokens exist in the lexer: `jl4-core/src/L4/Lexer.hs` lines 125–126 and 150–151 (`TEllipsis`, `TEllipsisOr`). "The surface" is the ICAIL draft's own word for the notation (line 157, note 18), introduced in the body where the block is explained.

[^11]: `paper/icail/l4-icail.tex` §Related Work: "Logical English … shares L4's controlled-natural-language ambition while remaining within logic programming; L4 differs in starting from typed functional programming and reaching the relational reading by transformation"; and, from line 992, "Catala … is the closest contemporary in spirit: a language for legislation, grounded in default logic, with a strong correctness story. L4 differs in its explicit constitutive/regulative split, its concurrency-aware contract semantics, and its CNL surface." "Runs as Prolog, which has no such step" is our gloss of "remaining within logic programming," and a statement about Prolog, which has no static type check and answers a query it cannot satisfy with failure; it is not a finding about Logical English's own tooling, which was not examined. The body's "declared or inferred": declaration is optional — a rule with a `GIVEN` and no `GIVETH` (``GIVEN x IS A NUMBER / DECIDE `big` x IF x > 10``) and a rule with neither (``  `answer` MEANS 42 ``) both print "Check succeeded" under `l4 check`, and a file whose second rule applies a `NUMBER`-typed predicate to `TRUE` fails the same check before anything is evaluated (probes run 2026-09-15 on the installed binary); an earlier revision said every rule "declares" its type, which overreached. "Refused at the desk" means at check time, before evaluation. Catala: Denis Merigoux, Nicolas Chataing and Jonathan Protzenko, "Catala: A Programming Language for the Law," *Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages* 5 (ICFP), 2021, DOI 10.1145/3473582; arXiv:2103.03198 (abstract page checked 2026-09-14), which reports evaluating the language on section 121 of the US federal income tax and on French family benefits, and in the latter uncovering "a bug in the official implementation." Affiliations are as post 1 records them from the byline (Inria; Inria and the École Normale Supérieure; Microsoft Research). The interleaving is as this repository's page on the language, `doc/exports/catala.md`, describes it: "you read the article, then immediately the rule derived from it." That form — formal text paired with the prose it serves — is the one Donald Knuth proposed for programs as literate programming in "Literate Programming," *The Computer Journal* 27(2):97–111 (1984), DOI 10.1093/comjnl/27.2.97, read in the author's preprint at <http://www.literateprogramming.com/knuthweb.pdf> (checked 2026-09-14), whose stated aim is "significantly better documentation of programs"; the resemblance is ours to draw, and whether Catala's authors cite Knuth was not checked today. The exports: `doc/exports/README.md` lists `l4 catala` and `l4 blawx` among five shipped backends (docassemble, OpenFisca, Catala, Blawx, DMN/BPMN), which is what "among others" covers. Blawx, which an earlier revision named in the body, is described in `doc/exports/blawx.md` as "a web-based, visual rules-as-code tool aimed at people who know the law but do not write code," in which "you assemble rules from drag-and-drop blocks in the browser," with s(CASP) underneath; the page names no author, so none is named here.

