[^15]: "Since at least 1991": Jordi Cabot, "Positioning of the low-code movement within the field of model-driven engineering," MODELS '20 Companion (ACM, 2020), DOI 10.1145/3417990.3420210; author's PDF at <https://modeling-languages.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Models2020_LowCodeWorkshop-2.pdf> (checked 2026-09-14): "Already in 1991, in the 1st edition of the well-known CAiSE conference, we could find papers stating concepts like: 'Given the final model, the complete computerized information system can be automatically generated' [3]"; his [3] is Gulla, Lindland and Willumsen, CAiSE '91, not opened, so the 1991 sentence is known at second hand. Cabot's emphasis is on low-code as a rebranding of model-driven engineering; the ceiling argument is not his. Threshold and ceiling: Brad Myers, Scott E. Hudson and Randy Pausch, "Past, Present, and Future of User Interface Software Tools," *ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction* 7(1):3–28 (March 2000), PDF at <https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~amulet/papers/futureofhciACM.pdf> (checked 2026-09-14): "The 'threshold' is how difficult it is to learn how to use the system, and the 'ceiling' is how much can be done using the system. The most successful current systems seem to be either low threshold and low ceiling, or high threshold and high ceiling." "The customers who needed the ceiling wrote code" is our summary of the low-code and model-driven cycles, not any source's; "since at least 1991" and the threshold-and-ceiling vocabulary were in the body of earlier revisions and now live only here. An earlier draft also said in the body that model-driven engineering lost ground because its models went stale, unread, while the code shipped; no source in this apparatus makes that argument, so it is recorded here as our reading and asserted nowhere.

[^16]: Nils Holzenberger, Andrew Blair-Stanek and Benjamin Van Durme, "A Dataset for Statutory Reasoning in Tax Law Entailment and Question Answering," arXiv:2005.05257, submitted 11 May 2020 (abstract page checked 2026-09-14): "The application of legislation to facts of a case we refer to as statutory reasoning"; "Straightforward application of machine reading models exhibits low out-of-the-box performance on our questions … We contrast this with a hand-constructed Prolog-based system, designed to fully solve the task." It is a benchmark for applying a statute to facts, released as entailment and question-answering tasks, and not a benchmark for translating statute into logic, which an earlier revision of the body wrongly called it; the dataset's name, SARA, and its workshop venue are not on the arXiv abstract page and are not asserted. Andrew Blair-Stanek, Nils Holzenberger and Benjamin Van Durme, "Can GPT-3 Perform Statutory Reasoning?," arXiv:2302.06100, submitted 13 February 2023 (abstract page checked 2026-09-14): the model was text-davinci-003; on the 2020 dataset "we achieve results with GPT-3 that are better than the previous best published results," and separately "we create simple synthetic statutes, which GPT-3 is guaranteed not to have seen during training. We find GPT-3 performs poorly at answering straightforward questions about these simple synthetic statutes." An earlier revision of the body attached "performs poorly" to the 2020 benchmark, which is the opposite of what the abstract reports for it; the body now names only the benchmark, and both of the 2023 results are here. Neither full text was read today.

[^17]: James K. Wiles, "Implementing Computational Law in Wolfram Language for the Governance of Artificial Intelligence," arXiv:2608.13958, submitted 14 August 2026; abstract at <https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13958> and full text at <https://arxiv.org/html/2608.13958> (both checked 2026-09-14). The formalism is Reified Input/Output Logic, "the formalism behind the DAPRECO knowledge base," implemented in Wolfram Language; the model is GPT-4, named in the abstract; OpenAI released GPT-4 in March 2023, so it was three years old at the paper's submission, which is the same caveat note 23 makes for METR's early-2025 tools. §3.7 gives the rule the body calls Statement A: "Those who are not wearing a tie or those who are blond ought to leave the room." §4.2: the generated `applyLegalRule` "misuses the pattern-condition operator /; outside of any pattern, so its two conditions never evaluate to anything. The final If then compares an unevaluated symbolic Or against Undefined, which always fails, so the function returns True for every input. Three of the four tests happen to expect True, so the suite looks healthy. The one discriminating case sits in plain sight in the first row: Alice, wearing a tie, not blond, wrongly ordered from the room. … Neither GPT-4 nor I noticed the bug at the time." The rule is `notWearingTie OR isBlond -> mustLeaveRoom`, so True means the obligation fires; "it ordered everyone out of the room" is what returning True for every input does here, and "one discriminating case, in its first row" is the sentence just quoted. §4.6, Table 1: Statements A, C and D "accepted as correct; actually wrong"; B "functionally correct"; the warm-up "ran only after inventing an API"; the paper adds that "the silent errors in Statements A, C, and D were found only during the preparation of this paper." §6.4: "The authoring experience should work like modern code assistance: formal building blocks that carry human-language representations, suggested inline as a lawyer drafts, with the surrounding document as context, the way Copilot and grammar checkers work today." "In his sketch the English draft is a prompt … with nothing to catch a guard" is our reading of that proposal, not his words. Four statements, one author, one formalism, one model, no control condition: a demonstration, not a rate, which is what the body's "four statements, one model" carries.

[^18]: `paper/icail/l4-icail.tex`, read in the worktree 2026-09-14; "our own draft" in the body is this file. Line 157: the surface is "deliberately engineered as a controlled natural language." §Motivation (line 230): "The role of the LLM, in our architecture, is not to replace this notation but to help author and explain it: to draft a first formalisation from source text, and to verbalise a rigorous evaluation trace back to the user in accessible prose." §Ingestion, "By hand, but better" (lines 797–808): "We have found this bridge to bear real weight: non-technical business stakeholders are able to *read* an L4 ruleset and *sign off* on it as a faithful statement of the rules they intend." The limitation, lines 947–951, quoted in the body with its middle clause — "between matching the structure of the text and matching the intent behind it" — elided; the body's gloss of *isomorphic* at that point is ours, in the sense note 20's source gives the word. Appendix, "A clause-by-clause walkthrough" (line 1140), quoted in the body. The paper is a draft; the body attributes its claims to it and does not assert them independently, and the sign-off claim is carried in the draft's own words — "are able to" — rather than as a completed event. The draft names no engagement, no number of readers and no procedure. Meng supplied them on 2026-09-14, answering the mark this note carried, in these words: "This is a qualitative impression; a quantitative study would have to be focus of future research similar to https://popl22.sigplan.org/details/prolala-2022-papers/7/Cod-e-ifying-The-Law. The qualitative impression is traceable to the work on the PDPA case and the insurance case; in both cases the 'client' assigned a business executive to review the encoding and even though they were not a programmer they were able to review and sign off." The two cases are the ones `blog/posts/01-race-condition-in-a-law.md` tells and dates: the Personal Data Protection Act pilot of 2022 and the insurer's policy of 2023. The body renders the account and no more — one executive per engagement, so two readers, and no procedure on record. [NEEDS MENG: the two executives — their roles, what each was shown (the L4, a rendering, a walkthrough), what each asked, and what signing off consisted of; the body has one sentence for both.] An earlier revision of this note carried a different account, unsourced, in which the encodings were reviewed with the commissioning bodies and with law students; it is replaced by the answer above. The pointer, opened 2026-09-14 (HTTP 200): Nel Escher, Jeffrey Bilik, Alexander Miller, Jennifer Jiyoung Huseby, Divya Ramesh, Alice Liu, Sam Mikell, Nina Cahill, Ben Green and Nikola Banovic, all of the University of Michigan, "Cod(e)ifying The Law," ProLaLa 2022 (Programming Languages and the Law), a workshop of POPL 2022, Philadelphia, 16 January 2022. Its abstract: "We discuss preliminary results from an ongoing experimental study recruiting individuals and teams of experts in computer science and law to each develop a tool that interprets a legal statute and advises layperson users about the statute's applicability to their case. We find considerable variation in how legal experts and programmers understand and encode the law, the resources they draw upon, and their confidence in implementing legal tools." The body's description of the study is from that abstract; the slides and extended abstract attached to the page were not opened, and whether the study was completed or published since was not checked. "Nothing in our software checks isomorphism": `etc/go/phases/p5-gate.sh` lists "isomorphism spot-checks — HG1 — no checkable form" and `etc/go/phases/p3-check.sh` says isomorphism "is unverified by this stage and is HG1's subject"; no script or test in the tree evaluates it (grep for `isomorph` across the tree's scripts and Haskell, 2026-09-14).

[^19]: Two older designs sit on either side of this sentence. Ian Grigg's Ricardian contract is a single document "easily readable by people (like a contract on paper)" and "readable by programs (parsable like a database)," a form he reports "in successful use since 1996" in the Ricardo payment system; <https://iang.org/papers/ricardian_contract.html> (checked 2026-09-14). Nick Szabo's 1996 essay "Smart Contracts: Building Blocks for Digital Markets" defines a smart contract as "a set of promises, specified in digital form, including protocols within which the parties perform on these promises"; read at <https://nakamotoinstitute.org/library/smart-contracts-building-blocks-for-digital-markets/> (checked 2026-09-14). L4's shape is Grigg's, one text read two ways, and not Szabo's, in which the code displaces the text.

[^20]: T. J. M. Bench-Capon and F. P. Coenen, "Isomorphism and Legal Knowledge Based Systems," *Artificial Intelligence and Law* 1:65–86 (1992). Read at <https://cgi.csc.liv.ac.uk/~tbc/publications/AILawIsomorphism.pdf> (checked 2026-09-14; the PDF has a text layer). Abstract: the paper "recommends isomorphism, the well defined correspondence of the knowledge base to the source texts, as a basic principle of system construction in the legal domain." "Knowledge base" is their term for the encoded rules; the body leaves the term as they wrote it. The paper does not coin the term: its §2 says "the term 'isomorphism' was used in works such as [Bench-Capon, 1989], [Karpf, 1989], [Vey Mestdagh, 1990] and [Bench-Capon and Forder, 1991]," which is why the body says "argued for" and not "coined." The Jackson quotation on p. 65 is from his *Principles of Program Design* (1975): "The beginning of wisdom for a programmer is to recognize the difference between getting his program to work and getting it right."

[^21]: William Welsh, "Oracle to buy RuleBurst," *Washington Technology*, 31 October 2008, <https://washingtontechnology.com/2008/10/oracle-to-buy-ruleburst/318958/> (checked 2026-09-14): RuleBurst Holdings was the parent of Haley Limited, and "Haley's automated policy platform translates legislation and policies into defined, automated rules for various applications," offering "social services agencies a comprehensive software package"; financial services and insurance also used it. Oracle's own press release and acquisition page both returned HTTP 403 today. The product's later names and its government users are from Wikipedia, "Oracle Intelligent Advisor" (formerly, and reachable as, "Oracle Policy Automation"), <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_Policy_Automation> (checked 2026-09-14): "Oracle Corporation acquired OPA in December 2008 when it purchased the Australian software company RuleBurst Holdings, then trading as Haley" — the acquisition was announced on 31 October and completed in December, which is why the body dates Oracle's ownership from December 2008 rather than saying it has sold the product since 2008; the renaming to Oracle Intelligent Advisor in December 2019; "the UK Revenue and Customs agency uses it for their Employment Status Indicator assessment tool," citing an agency impact assessment; "the United States IRS uses the software for guidance on tax law," citing a Treasury document; and "In France, the CNAF uses Oracle Intelligent Advisor for benefit calculations." The body names none of the users; they are here, in the article's present tense, with none of the agency documents opened. "Oracle has owned one since 2008" is the December 2008 completion; an earlier revision named the British use and the Word-styled rules in the body, and both are now here only. "Write your rules in plain English": Oracle Policy Modeling documentation, "Write rules in Word," <https://docs.oracle.com/html/E79061_01/Content/Writing%20rules/Write_rules_in_Word.htm> (checked 2026-09-14): "Using Microsoft Word you can write your rules in plain English. You then format these rules with the styles provided on the Oracle Policy Modeling tab to enable them to be compiled into a format that can be used by the Oracle Determinations Engine." Why it did not become the drafting office's tool — bought downstream of enactment, proprietary engine, a second artifact — is our argument, not a finding of any source, and so is the claim that such a rulebase drifts when the statute is amended.

